            <ArticleTitle>Chronic relapsing myalgia (? Post viral): clinical, histological, and biochemical studies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>305-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Two patients with persistent myalgia characterised by onset after an ill-defined systemic illness, marked fluctuations in the severity of the symptoms, and normal neuromuscular examination with the exception of variable muscle tenderness on deep palpation, may have a forme fruste of myalgic encephalomyelitis. Differentiation from psychogenic muscle pain is important in management. Muscle histology revealed non-specific Type II fibre atrophy. Mitochondrial respiration was assayed polarographically in intact organelles in vitro and revealed a mild depression of State 3 respiration rates with Site I and Site II substrates.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
